  <div class="bio">
	<p>
    From <a href="https://jupiter.bluesharkfriends.com">Levi Jupiter</a>
	</p>
	<br />
	<p>
	Elizabeth Casswell, age 50, passed away on December 25, 2025, in Denver, Colorado. Born on December 30, 1974, in Colorado, Elizabeth lived a life defined by compassion, advocacy, and an unwavering commitment to justice and dignity for all.
	</p>
	<br />
	<p>
	Throughout her life, Elizabeth dedicated herself to empowering disabled individuals and championing their rights. Her professional work with nonprofits focused on supporting people with disabilities, ensuring they were treated with respect and that their autonomy was honored. She was deeply committed to the principle of self-determination—the belief that every person has the right to make decisions about their own life, regardless of disability, mental health status, or age. This belief was not just a philosophy for Elizabeth; it was a guiding force in her work and her life.
	</p>
	<br />
	<p>
	Elizabeth’s activism extended far beyond her professional roles. A lifelong advocate for social justice, she was often found at protests and community events, standing up for the rights of marginalized communities. During the last four years of her life, she co-founded and co-managed a disability justice Facebook page that became a vital platform for raising awareness and uplifting disabled voices. Her efforts helped foster understanding and solidarity within the disability community and beyond.
	</p>
	<br />
	<p>
	Her time in college was marked by her leadership in the Gay-Straight Alliance, where she played a key role in building a strong and inclusive queer community. Her passion for creating safe spaces and advocating for LGBTQ+ rights left a lasting impact on countless individuals.
	</p>
	<br />
	<p>
	Elizabeth was also known for her deep love of learning. She pursued knowledge with enthusiasm throughout her life—not only for personal growth but also to better support those around her. Whether researching autism, mental health issues, or navigating complex medical and disability systems, she used what she learned to uplift others. She freely shared resources and information with anyone who needed them, often helping people access services they might not have found otherwise.
	</p>
	<br />
	<p>
	Her generosity extended to the animal world as well. Elizabeth had an immense love for animals and spent years rescuing and reuniting lost pets with their families. Her home was often a temporary haven for animals in need, reflecting her boundless compassion.
	</p>
	<br />
	<p>
	Elizabeth’s friendships were marked by fierce loyalty and genuine care. She was a loving and generous friend who always sought to understand others more deeply so she could support them better. Her laugh was infectious—brightening even the darkest days—and she never failed to find joy or humor, no matter the circumstances.
	</p>
	<br />
	<p>
	She also had a playful side: she loved video games and approached them with both skill and a lovingly competitive spirit. Music was another passion of hers; she often shared songs as a way to connect with others.
	</p>
	<br />
	<p>
	The impact of Elizabeth’s life is profound and far-reaching. Through her advocacy, education efforts, activism, friendships, and resource-sharing, she touched countless lives. Those who knew her continue to carry forward the knowledge and compassion she so freely gave—spreading it further into communities near and far.
	</p>
	<br />
	<p>
	Though words can never fully capture the breadth of her influence or the depth of her love, Elizabeth’s legacy lives on in every person she empowered, every voice she uplifted, every animal she rescued, and every act of kindness inspired by her example.
	</p>
	<br />
	<p>
	May her memory be a source of strength and love to all who knew her.
	</p>

